    <h2><code>@​graphiql/toolkit</code><a
    href="https://github.com/1"><code>@​1</code></a>.0.0</h2>
    <h3>Major Changes</h3>
    <ul>
    <li>
    <p><a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/graphql/graphiql/pull/4478">#4478</a>
    <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/commit/066528a6bb7706d685536888d7b5549d6fd5a109"><code>066528a</code></a>
    Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/trevor-scheer"><code>@​trevor-scheer</code></a>!
    - GraphQL.js 15 and 16.0–16.10 are no longer supported peer
    dependencies. The supported range is <code>^16.11.0 || ^17.0.0</code>.
    GraphQL.js 16.11 fixes OneOf input validation for nullable variables and
    tightens input-object coercion to reject arrays, giving GraphiQL 6 a
    correct baseline for OneOf inputs. Upgrade <code>graphql</code> before
    upgrading these packages.</p>
    </li>
    <li>
    <p><a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/graphql/graphiql/pull/4392">#4392</a>
    <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/commit/26ae143ba68004d4a50468ba1feb649ace673f3a"><code>26ae143</code></a>
    Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/trevor-scheer"><code>@​trevor-scheer</code></a>!
    - Remove the deprecated <code>legacyClient</code> alias from
    <code>CreateFetcherOptions</code>. It duplicated
    <code>legacyWsClient</code> — pass <code>legacyWsClient</code>
    instead.</p>
    </li>
    </ul>
    <h3>Minor Changes</h3>
    <ul>
    <li>
    <p><a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/graphql/graphiql/pull/4333">#4333</a>
    <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/commit/093cb100a4524b1005b82c1c064bb897416bfc82"><code>093cb10</code></a>
    Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/trevor-scheer"><code>@​trevor-scheer</code></a>!
    - Add <code>createTransport</code> and a structured
    <code>Transport</code> API for queries, mutations, subscriptions, and
    incremental delivery. Pass a transport through <code>&lt;GraphiQL
    transport={...}&gt;</code>; the <code>transport</code> and
    <code>fetcher</code> props are mutually exclusive at the type level.</p>
    <p><code>GraphiQLProps</code> is now a union. If a TypeScript wrapper
    uses <code>interface AppProps extends GraphiQLProps</code>, replace it
    with a type intersection such as <code>type AppProps = GraphiQLProps
    &amp; { applicationName: string }</code>, even when retaining
    <code>fetcher</code>. See the <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/blob/c2e792bc446d5d6751bd7cd1d9ac283a4a1d8fc4/docs/migration/graphiql-6.0.0.md#overview">migration
    overview</a>.</p>
    <p>When <code>supportedMethods</code> is omitted,
    <code>createTransport</code> discovers endpoint capabilities through
    <code>OPTIONS</code> and the <code>Allow</code> header. Queries prefer
    QUERY, then POST, then GET. Mutations and operations of unknown type
    require POST. An explicit <code>method</code> override applies to
    queries and fails if the endpoint excludes it. GET sends GraphQL
    parameters in the URL; POST and QUERY send a JSON body.</p>
    <p>Discovery is shared by concurrent requests and repeats when effective
    request headers change. If discovery fails, exceeds two seconds, or the
    <code>Allow</code> header is missing or malformed, the transport assumes
    only its initially configured <code>method</code> (POST by default) is
    available. A valid advertisement containing no supported methods blocks
    execution. Pass a nonempty <code>supportedMethods</code> array to skip
    discovery for known capabilities. Cross-origin servers must permit
    OPTIONS and expose <code>Allow</code> through
    <code>Access-Control-Expose-Headers</code>.</p>
    <p><code>TransportResponse</code> exposes HTTP method, status, headers,
    timing, and byte sizes alongside the GraphQL result. HTTP metadata
    remains available when a response body is not valid JSON. Its
    <code>ok</code> field accounts for both HTTP status and GraphQL errors.
    <code>TransportRequest</code> supports GraphQL <code>extensions</code>
    and an abort <code>signal</code>. Subscriptions require an explicit
    <code>subscriptionClient</code> with an <code>iterate(request)</code>
    method, supported by <code>graphql-ws</code> v6 and
    <code>graphql-sse</code> clients.</p>
    <p>Plugins can transform outgoing requests with
    <code>onBeforeSend</code> and observe responses or request failures with
    <code>onResponse</code> and <code>onError</code>, available through
    <code>useGraphiQLPluginContext().transport</code>. Each registration
    returns a cleanup function. This context field is absent when using the
    legacy <code>fetcher</code> prop.</p>
    <p>Transports can expose query method preferences through optional
    <code>setMethod</code>, <code>methodOverride</code>, and
    <code>subscribe(listener)</code> APIs. Calling <code>setMethod()</code>
    without an argument restores automatic selection.
    <code>methodOverride</code> distinguishes an explicit preference from
    Auto; <code>Transport.method</code> reflects the query method, while
    <code>TransportResponse.method</code> reports the method actually used
    for an execution. <code>subscribe(listener)</code> notifies GraphiQL of
    preference and capability changes and returns an unsubscribe function.
    These notifications are independent of request lifecycle hooks.
    <code>createTransport</code> implements these APIs; custom transports
    can omit them.</p>
    <p><code>createGraphiQLFetcher</code>, <code>Fetcher</code>, and
    <code>&lt;GraphiQL fetcher={...}&gt;</code> are deprecated but remain
    supported. See the <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/blob/c2e792bc446d5d6751bd7cd1d9ac283a4a1d8fc4/docs/migration/graphiql-6.0.0.md#new-transport-api">Transport
    migration guide</a> for examples and custom transport requirements.</p>
    </li>
    </ul>
    <h2><code>@​graphiql/toolkit</code><a
    href="https://github.com/1"><code>@​1</code></a>.0.0-rc.1</h2>
    <h3>Patch Changes</h3>
    <ul>
    <li>
    <p><a
    href="https://redirect.github.com/graphql/graphiql/pull/4665">#4665</a>
    <a
    href="https://github.com/graphql/graphiql/commit/c4a62afd30d858c51da233b3f7c7283a35e4d5d2"><code>c4a62af</code></a>
    Thanks <a
    href="https://github.com/trevor-scheer"><code>@​trevor-scheer</code></a>!
    - Discover HTTP methods automatically when <code>createTransport</code>
    is called without <code>supportedMethods</code>. Previous RCs treated an
    omitted <code>supportedMethods</code> as <code>['POST']</code> and
    exposed a method chip and switcher in the top bar. The transport now
    sends an OPTIONS request without a body before its first HTTP operation,
    reads <code>Allow</code>, and automatically selects QUERY, then POST,
    then GET for queries. The top bar method controls are removed. The
    response pane instead shows the actual request method from the optional
    <code>TransportResponse.method</code> field alongside timing and size,
    including for HTTP error responses and incremental chunks. WebSocket
    events and custom transports without this metadata omit the method.</p>
    <p>Discovery uses the effective request headers, is shared by concurrent
    requests, and runs again when headers change. If OPTIONS fails, times
    out after two seconds, or returns no usable <code>Allow</code> header,
    execution falls back to POST (or the configured <code>method</code>
    override). A valid advertisement containing no usable methods blocks
    HTTP execution. Mutations always require POST; GraphiQL delegates
    routing to the transport from Run, the operation dropdown, and the
    keyboard shortcut. An operation whose type cannot be determined also
    requires POST. If POST is unavailable, these operations fail without
    sending.</p>
    <p>Pass <code>method: 'GET'</code> to override automatic query selection
    for testing. GET encodes queries, operation names, variables, and
    extensions in the URL without a body; mutations still require POST. An
    override that discovery excludes fails without silently choosing another
    method. <code>transport.setMethod?.('GET')</code> changes the override
    for subsequent queries, and <code>transport.setMethod?.()</code>
    restores automatic selection. Pass <code>supportedMethods:
    ['POST']</code> to preserve the previous POST-only default and skip
    OPTIONS, or use <code>method: 'GET', supportedMethods: ['GET',
    'POST']</code> to test GET queries and POST mutations without discovery.
    If discovery fails with a configured GET override and no explicit
    capabilities, only GET is assumed available.</p>
    <p>For cross-origin discovery, the server must permit OPTIONS and expose
    <code>Allow</code> through <code>Access-Control-Expose-Headers</code>.
    This discovers methods only, not QUERY request formats through
    <code>Accept-Query</code>, and does not retry operations with another
    method. Custom transports own routing and must prevent mutations over
    GET and QUERY. GraphiQL reads metadata from each response without
    subscribing to transport capability changes.</p>
    </li>
    </ul>
